  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic of homologous recombination between two similar DNA duplexes carrying different alleles, shown in blue and red. It starts with aligned parental molecules labeled with lowercase and uppercase versions of the same marker, then shows strand breakage and strand invasion, producing a crossover-like intermediate with heteroduplex DNA where strands from the two parents pair despite carrying mismatched bases.\n\nIn the central step, the crossed DNA structure appears to be a Holliday junction, with short regions containing opposite-color segments that mark heteroduplex tracts. Green arrows indicate branch migration or junction movement, which can expand these mixed regions. The key message is that recombination can yield different patterns of allele segregation depending on how mismatch repair resolves the heteroduplexes.\n\nThe outcomes on the right compare two possible genetic ratios. One outcome gives a normal 2:2 segregation, where each allele is recovered twice. The other gives a 3:1 pattern, indicating gene conversion, where one allele replaces the other in one recombinant product. Overall, the figure explains how meiotic recombination coupled to mismatch repair of heteroduplex DNA can convert one allele to another instead of preserving simple reciprocal exchange.",
